WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.850
Move over, Doctor Strange — there's a new
Sorcerer in town.

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:05.450
"We're all gonna die!"

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.390
The MCU's Peter Parker may feel like he has
the weight of the world on his shoulders,

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.390
but at least he has a few good friends watching
his back.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:17.240
Peter's friend Ned proves to be exceptionally
useful in the Holland-led threequel "Spider-Man:

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:18.240
No Way Home."

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.269
It's not just his intelligence that helps
Peter conquer the day; in this movie, it's

00:00:22.269 --> 00:00:24.679
his magical hands — literally.

00:00:24.679 --> 00:00:28.989
Ned must have some sorcerers somewhere in
his family tree because once he attains a

00:00:28.989 --> 00:00:33.030
Sling Ring — the device Doctor Strange uses
to create portals — he's able to use it

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.590
all on his own, doing it just about perfectly
on his first try.

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:40.710
To say this comes in handy in the movie is
a pretty major understatement.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:45.090
Matter of fact, his sorcery directly leads
to the movie's coolest moments.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:49.670
It's safe to say hardly anyone had "Ned becomes
a sorcerer" on their "Spider-Man: No Way Home"

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.879
bingo card, but that's precisely what we got.

00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:56.219
At the very least, Ned becomes incredibly
proficient at using a Sling Ring in a short

00:00:56.219 --> 00:00:57.219
amount of time.

00:00:57.219 --> 00:01:01.879
Whereas it took Stephen Strange quite a while
to get the hang of making portals, Peter Parker's

00:01:01.879 --> 00:01:06.120
right-hand man figures out how to do it almost
immediately -- although he apparently still

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.250
needs to learn how not to immediately close
them after somebody walks through.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:14.190
It's a fun addition to the "Spider-Man" canon
and plays a significant role in several moments

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:15.190
throughout the film.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:19.280
The most notable instance of Ned's Sling Ring
proficiency coming in handy is when he and

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.940
MJ want to find their Peter.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:25.250
But when he merely says "Peter Parker," we
get Andrew Garfield's version of the character

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:26.420
and then Tobey Maguire's.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:27.580
"Pizza time."

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.140
It's a fun way to bring all of the Spider-Men
into one place.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:35.400
However, whether Ned's set to join the ranks
of the sorcerers himself in the future is

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.940
still very much up in the air.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:41.910
During the heartwrenching "No Way Home" finale,
Holland's Peter asks Doctor Strange to make

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:44.660
everyone in the universe forget about Peter
Parker.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:49.070
He doesn't merely ask to make people forget
Peter is Spider-Man; he asks that Strange

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:52.110
wipe out his existence from peoples' memories
completely.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.970
That means Ned forgets all of his memories
of Peter, too.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:59.570
By that logic, it would make sense for him
also to forget he can tap into magical powers.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.500
Who knows if he'll ever remember the power
he holds in the palm of his hands.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:08.280
Ned being a magic user isn't just a fun thing
they added to Ned's character for the movies.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:13.140
His character's namesake from the comics,
Ned Leeds, has a villainous alter-ego as one

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.980
version of the Hobgoblin — one who's also
proficient in sorcery.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.340
This is exemplified in the storyline for "Symbiote
Spider-Man: Alien Reality."

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:27.130
In it, Ned, a.k.a. Hobgoblin, decides to work
alongside Baron Mordo, who's played by Chiwetel

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.270
Ejiofor in the MCU.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:33.810
Hobgoblin steals a magic book at the Sanctum
Sanctorum called the Word of God, which allows

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.960
the user to alter the universe.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:41.090
Through this, Hobgoblin's able to make Mordo
the Sorcerer Supreme while Ned becomes his

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:42.090
apprentice.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.530
Suffice to say, nothing like that happens
in "No Way Home."

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:48.910
While Ned learns how to use some magic, it's
limited to making portals via a sling ring.

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:54.150
He doesn't go full evil just yet, but there
is a line to suggest Ned could become Hobgoblin

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:55.250
down the road.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:59.610
After Tom Holland's Peter Parker meets the
other two, Tobey Maguire's version tells Ned

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.930
about how he fought his own best friend, Harry
Osborn.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:06.840
"Look at little Goblin Jr. Gonna cry?"

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:11.350
The story seems to really stick with Ned,
and he immediately turns to Tom Holland's

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:15.170
Spider-Man to inform him that he'll never
turn evil and fight him.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:19.319
It's a fun line, especially for those who
are aware of Ned's comic book alter-ego.

00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:23.590
And with Baron Mordo returning to the MCU
with a villainous role in "Doctor Strange

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:26.490
in the Multiverse of Madness," who knows what
could transpire?

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.810
Perhaps the two characters will meet one day
in the MCU after all.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:35.349
"You're an F.O.S. now — 'friend of Spider-Man'
– and you have to remember, just stay calm."

